  <!-- ── Where Accountium Wins Divider ────────────────────── -->
  <section class="b4s2" aria-labelledby="wins-heading">
    <h2 id="wins-heading">Where Accountium Wins</h2>
    <p>
      The clearest advantages come down to pricing structure and how the product
      holds together as a single suite.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Where Accountium Wins Content ────────────────────── -->
  <section class="content-section bg-subtle" aria-label="Accountium advantages">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="feature-block">
        <ul class="criteria-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Payroll without the add-on tax</strong>
            <p>QuickBooks sells payroll as a separate paid tier on top of your subscription. In Accountium, payroll runs, direct deposit, and tax filings are part of the core product — one login, one bill, no upsell path to unlock the feature you actually need.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>One suite, not a bundle of upsells</strong>
            <p>Invoicing, banking, payroll, and reporting live in a single interface built to work together. You're not navigating a base product with a menu of paid add-ons layered on top.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Built for how you actually work</strong>
            <p>Real-time bank feed categorization and a mobile experience that mirrors the desktop app, so you're not stuck waiting for sync or hunting for a feature that only exists on one platform.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Multi-currency on every plan</strong>
            <p>QuickBooks reserves multi-currency for higher tiers. In Accountium, it's included — no upgrade required to invoice or report in USD, EUR, GBP, or 30+ other currencies.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Where QuickBooks Wins Divider ────────────────────── -->
  <section class="b4s2" aria-labelledby="honest-heading">
    <h2 id="honest-heading">Where QuickBooks Is the Safer Bet</h2>
    <p>
      Honest assessment: QuickBooks has real advantages that are worth weighing
      depending on your situation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Where QuickBooks Wins Content ────────────────────── -->
  <section class="content-section" aria-label="QuickBooks advantages">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="feature-block">
        <div class="callout callout--tip">
          <strong>A bigger ecosystem</strong>
          QuickBooks has been around far longer and has a much larger marketplace of
          third-party integrations and a bigger network of accountants and bookkeepers
          already fluent in it. If your bookkeeper specifically works in QuickBooks,
          or you depend on a niche integration, that's a real switching cost worth
          weighing.
        </div>
        <ul class="feature-list">
          <li>750+ third-party app integrations</li>
          <li>Large certified ProAdvisor network</li>
          <li>Longer track record in the market</li>
          <li>Widely used by accountants and bookkeepers</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Pricing Divider ───────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="b4s2" aria-labelledby="pricing-heading">
    <h2 id="pricing-heading">Pricing</h2>
    <p>
      The core difference isn't a dollar figure — it's a structural one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Pricing Content ──────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="content-section bg-subtle" aria-label="Pricing comparison">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="feature-block">
        <h3 class="feature-block-heading">One Bill vs. Many</h3>
        <p class="feature-block-body">
          Accountium hasn't published set pricing tiers yet. The core difference in
          approach: QuickBooks prices by feature tier, with payroll and some advanced
          features sold as add-ons. Accountium is built around a single plan with
          everything included, so your bill doesn't grow every time you need another
          feature.
        </p>
        <div class="callout callout--tip">
          <strong>Want current pricing?</strong>
          <a href="/contact">Get in touch</a> — no pricing page runaround. We'll give
          you a straight answer based on your business size.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Switching Divider ─────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="b4s2" aria-labelledby="switching-heading">
    <h2 id="switching-heading">Switching from QuickBooks</h2>
    <p>
      Moving your books over is a straightforward three-step process. Most
      businesses complete the migration in a single afternoon.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Switching Content ────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="content-section" aria-label="Migration steps">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="feature-block">

        <!-- ── Switching Flow graphic ── -->
        <div class="sw-embed">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 640 300" width="100%" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet" role="img" aria-label="Switching to Accountium in three easy steps: export, import, run a parallel period">
            <defs><clipPath id="swCardClip"><rect x="0.5" y="0.5" width="639" height="299" rx="14"/></clipPath></defs>
            <g clip-path="url(#swCardClip)"><rect x="0" y="0" width="640" height="300" fill="#FBF8F3"/></g>
            <rect x="0.5" y="0.5" width="639" height="299" rx="14" fill="none" stroke="#E8E2D8"/>
            <line x1="0" y1="48" x2="640" y2="48" stroke="#E8E2D8"/>
            <circle cx="26" cy="24" r="6" fill="#E07A5F"/>
            <circle cx="44" cy="24" r="6" fill="#E2B33C"/>
            <circle cx="62" cy="24" r="6" fill="#2F6B4E"/>
            <text x="320" y="90" text-anchor="middle" class="sans" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="#1A1714">Switching takes three steps</text>
            <line x1="148" y1="160" x2="292" y2="160" stroke="#D7CFC2" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="3 5"/>
            <line x1="388" y1="160" x2="532" y2="160" stroke="#D7CFC2" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="3 5"/>
            <circle class="sw-dot sw-dot--1" r="4"/>
